{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c527","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a077c8-d88a-7931-843c-3e9dddbe9444"},"sequence":238,"recorded_at":1780531400000471,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68"},"call_id":"call_01a077c94be070a09dd9b774965237dd","text":"Read text file `internal/secconftest/doc.go`.\n1|// Package secconftest is the conformance-test instrument for the\n2|// security-primitives-and-conformance-ci story: deterministic fixture\n3|// runners, fake clocks, crash points, protocol fuzz drivers, hostile\n4|// strings, and platform capability skips over the Section 16 production\n5|// gates (internal/secprim).\n6|//\n7|// It is an instrument, not a second authority. Every behavior it covers\n8|// is decided by an existing owner; this package only drives those\n9|// decisions deterministically and proves the drive reaches them:\n10|//\n11|//   - path grammars and bounds stay in internal/scalar;\n12|//   - wire decoding, string measure, and bound checks stay in\n13|//     internal/environ;\n14|//   - argv, environment, redaction, escaping, and containment refusals\n15|//     stay in internal/secprim, whose production code this package must\n16|//     not change;\n17|//   - the pinned specification text stays in internal/specdoc, which\n18|//     this package reads only to derive hostile classes, never to\n19|//     advertise product capabilities.\n20|//\n21|// Normative scope: AX v0.5.0 Sections 16, 19.2-19.5, 20.2, Appendix D.\n22|// Section 19.3 provider suites and Section 19.4 lifecycle cases outside\n23|// Section 16 are not driven here; the per-mechanism tests name that\n24|// bound where it bites.\n25|//\n26|// Stated bounds. The crash Transactor is a test model of a three-phase\n27|// durable commit, not the product journal: it proves the injector and\n28|// the outcome vocabulary work, not that any product recovery path\n29|// works. The five crash-point names are instrument-local phase\n30|// boundaries, not the SPEC CR-MAT-01..08 registry: registry-style\n31|// names are refused as unknown, and a new crash site added without an\n32|// arm is outside this model. Every arm the suite sets is\n33|// consumption-checked through RemainingArmed, so an armed point the\n34|// drive never reaches fails its test instead of passing silently. The\n35|// fuzz drivers cover the Section 16 string and vector gates only;\n36|// containment races and TOCTOU behavior are out of scope for\n37|// byte-mutating fuzz. The IsEnvName and Redact fuzz properties pin\n38|// the two poles a no-op gate fails (refused empty/admitted\n39|// well-formed name; rewritten secret/passing innocuous line); the\n40|// interior grammar and per-class markers stay covered by the hostile\n41|// corpus tests named on each property. Hostile members derive from the\n42|// specification text plus package-unicode tables and internal/scalar\n43|// predicates, so deleting a secprim gate arm keeps the corpus and\n44|// reddens the gate test instead of shrinking the witness with it. The\n45|// per-test skip datum is the `--- SKIP` lines under `go test -v`,\n46|// not the process-wide Report line, which only honestly witnesses\n47|// the verdicts the suite really recorded.\n48|package secconftest","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c529","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a077c8-d88a-7931-843c-3e9dddbe9444"},"sequence":239,"recorded_at":1780531400000473,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68"},"call_id":"call_01a077c94c8b7d9091d179badc09dd48","text":"Read text file `internal/secconftest/fuzz.go`.\n1|package secconftest\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"sync/atomic\"\n5|\n6|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/scalar\"\n7|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/secprim\"\n8|)\n9|\n10|// FuzzEntry identifies one production entry point a fuzz driver\n11|// reaches. FuzzTargetEntry maps each fuzz target to its entry so the\n12|// suite can assert the mapping instead of claiming it in prose.\n13|type FuzzEntry string\n14|\n15|const (\n16|\tEntryCheckArgv           FuzzEntry = \"secprim.CheckArgv\"\n17|\tEntryCheckMemberPath     FuzzEntry = \"secprim.CheckMemberPath\"\n18|\tEntryIsEnvName           FuzzEntry = \"secprim.IsEnvName\"\n19|\tEntryRedact              FuzzEntry = \"secprim.Redact\"\n20|\tEntryEscapeForTerminal   FuzzEntry = \"secprim.EscapeForTerminal\"\n21|\tEntryRenderForTerminal   FuzzEntry = \"secprim.RenderForTerminal\"\n22|\tEntryGuardResolve        FuzzEntry = \"secprim.Guard.Resolve\"\n23|\tEntryDetectCaseCollision FuzzEntry = \"secprim.DetectCaseCollision\"\n24|)\n25|\n26|// FuzzTargetEntry maps each committed fuzz target to the production\n27|// entry point it drives. Every target in fuzz_test.go must appear\n28|// here, and TestFuzzTargetsReachProduction pins both directions: no\n29|// target without an entry, no entry in the map without a target that\n30|// fires it.\n31|var FuzzTargetEntry = map[string]FuzzEntry{\n32|\t\"FuzzCheckArgv\":           EntryCheckArgv,\n33|\t\"FuzzCheckMemberPath\":     EntryCheckMemberPath,\n34|\t\"FuzzIsEnvName\":           EntryIsEnvName,\n35|\t\"FuzzRedactCorpus\":        EntryRedact,\n36|\t\"FuzzEscapeForTerminal\":   EntryEscapeForTerminal,\n37|\t\"FuzzRenderForTerminal\":   EntryRenderForTerminal,\n38|\t\"FuzzGuardResolve\":        EntryGuardResolve,\n39|\t\"FuzzDetectCaseCollision\": EntryDetectCaseCollision,\n40|}\n41|\n42|// Driver is the shared path from seed-corpus tests and fuzz targets\n43|// into the Section 16 production gates. Both call these methods, so\n44|// the seed tests prove arrival at the same entry points the fuzzer\n45|// mutates against. Every method records its call before delegating,\n46|// so a driver that stopped reaching production shows a zero count.\n47|type Driver struct {\n48|\tcalls map[FuzzEntry]*atomic.Int64\n49|}\n50|\n51|// NewDriver builds a Driver with zeroed arrival counters.\n52|func NewDriver() *Driver {\n53|\tdriver := &Driver{calls: map[FuzzEntry]*atomic.Int64{}}\n54|\tfor _, entry := range []FuzzEntry{\n55|\t\tEntryCheckArgv, EntryCheckMemberPath, EntryIsEnvName,\n56|\t\tEntryRedact, EntryEscapeForTerminal, EntryRenderForTerminal,\n57|\t\tEntryGuardResolve, EntryDetectCaseCollision,\n58|\t} {\n59|\t\tdriver.calls[entry] = &atomic.Int64{}\n60|\t}\n61|\treturn driver\n62|}\n63|\n64|func (driver *Driver) record(entry FuzzEntry) {\n65|\tdriver.calls[entry].Add(1)\n66|}\n67|\n68|// Count reports arrivals at entry.\n69|func (driver *Driver) Count(entry FuzzEntry) int64 {\n70|\tcounter, ok := driver.calls[entry]\n71|\tif !ok {\n72|\t\treturn 0\n73|\t}\n74|\treturn counter.Load()\n75|}\n76|\n77|// DriveArgv drives secprim.CheckArgv and reports admission.\n78|func (driver *Driver) DriveArgv(argv []string) bool {\n79|\tdriver.record(EntryCheckArgv)\n80|\treturn secprim.CheckArgv(argv) == nil\n81|}\n82|\n83|// DriveMemberPath drives secprim.CheckMemberPath on the linux member\n84|// grammar and reports admission.\n85|func (driver *Driver) DriveMemberPath(member string) bool {\n86|\tdriver.record(EntryCheckMemberPath)\n87|\treturn secprim.CheckMemberPath(scalar.PlatformLinux, member) == nil\n88|}\n89|\n90|// DriveEnvName drives secprim.IsEnvName.\n91|func (driver *Driver) DriveEnvName(name string) bool {\n92|\tdriver.record(EntryIsEnvName)\n93|\treturn secprim.IsEnvName(name)\n94|}\n95|\n96|// DriveRedact drives secprim.Redact with no caller corpus and reports\n97|// whether the output still carries the input's non-secret marker. The\n98|// fixed marker keeps the driver deterministic: corpus secrets come\n99|// from the caller in TestFuzzDriversReachProduction, not from the\n100|// fuzzer.\n101|func (driver *Driver) DriveRedact(line string) bool {\n102|\tdriver.record(EntryRedact)\n103|\tout := secprim.Redact(line, nil)\n104|\treturn len(out) > 0 || len(line) == 0\n105|}\n106|\n107|// DriveEscape drives secprim.EscapeForTerminal.\n108|func (driver *Driver) DriveEscape(text string) string {\n109|\tdriver.record(EntryEscapeForTerminal)\n110|\treturn secprim.EscapeForTerminal(text)\n111|}\n112|\n113|// DriveRender drives secprim.RenderForTerminal with no caller corpus.\n114|func (driver *Driver) DriveRender(line string) string {\n115|\tdriver.record(EntryRenderForTerminal)\n116|\treturn secprim.RenderForTerminal(line, nil)\n117|}\n118|\n119|// DriveGuardResolve drives secprim.Guard.Resolve against a fixed\n120|// in-memory root and reports admission. The guard construction cannot\n121|// fail for the fixed root; a construction failure refuses the input\n122|// the same way a resolution refusal does.\n123|func (driver *Driver) DriveGuardResolve(member string) bool {\n124|\tdriver.record(EntryGuardResolve)\n125|\tguard, err := secprim.NewGuard(\"/stage\", scalar.PlatformLinux, nil)\n126|\tif err != nil {\n127|\t\treturn false\n128|\t}\n129|\t_, err = guard.Resolve(member)\n130|\treturn err == nil\n131|}\n132|\n133|// DriveCaseCollision drives secprim.DetectCaseCollision against a\n134|// fixed sibling set and reports whether a collision fired.\n135|func (driver *Driver) DriveCaseCollision(candidate string) bool {\n136|\tdriver.record(EntryDetectCaseCollision)\n137|\t_, collided := secprim.DetectCaseCollision([]string{\"README.md\", \"staging/member\"}, candidate)\n138|\treturn collided\n139|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c52b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a077c8-d88a-7931-843c-3e9dddbe9444"},"sequence":240,"recorded_at":1780531400000475,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68"},"call_id":"call_01a077c94d5b7e23ae7b0260d9eab2d5","text":"Read text file `internal/secconftest/hostile.go`.\n1|package secconftest\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"fmt\"\n5|\t\"strings\"\n6|\n7|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/specdoc\"\n8|)\n9|\n10|// HostileClass is one spec-derived hostile-input class: the exact\n11|// specification phrase it comes from, the section that phrase lives\n12|// in, and the production gate that decides its members.\n13|//\n14|// The roster is derived, not listed: TestHostileRosterQuotesResolve\n15|// requires every Quote to occur in its Section in the pinned document,\n16|// so a class added to or removed from the specification fails the\n17|// roster instead of passing silently. Members are generated from\n18|// Unicode tables and internal/scalar predicates, never from the\n19|// secprim tables under test, so deleting a gate arm keeps the corpus\n20|// and reddens the gate test instead of shrinking the witness with it.\n21|type HostileClass struct {\n22|\t// ID is the stable slug naming the class in test output.\n23|\tID string\n24|\t// Section is the AX specification section the Quote is drawn from.\n25|\tSection string\n26|\t// Quote is the exact specification phrase deriving this class.\n27|\tQuote string\n28|\t// Gate names the production entry point deciding the members.\n29|\tGate string\n30|}\n31|\n32|// Roster lists every hostile class this instrument drives.\n33|func Roster() []HostileClass {\n34|\treturn []HostileClass{\n35|\t\t{ID: \"path-traversal\", Section: \"16.3\", Quote: \"path traversal\", Gate: \"secprim.CheckMemberPath\"},\n36|\t\t{ID: \"path-absolute\", Section: \"16.3\", Quote: \"absolute paths\", Gate: \"secprim.CheckMemberPath\"},\n37|\t\t{ID: \"path-drive-unc\", Section: \"16.3\", Quote: \"drive/UNC injection\", Gate: \"secprim.CheckMemberPath\"},\n38|\t\t{ID: \"path-altstream\", Section: \"16.3\", Quote: \"alternate streams\", Gate: \"secprim.CheckMemberPath\"},\n39|\t\t{ID: \"path-encoded-separator\", Section: \"16.3\", Quote: \"repeatedly encoded separators\", Gate: \"secprim.CheckMemberPath\"},\n40|\t\t{ID: \"path-symlink-escape\", Section: \"16.3\", Quote: \"symlink/reparse escape during both validation and commit\", Gate: \"secprim.Guard.Open\"},\n41|\t\t{ID: \"path-case-collision\", Section: \"16.3\", Quote: \"case-fold collisions on case-insensitive destinations\", Gate: \"secprim.DetectCaseCollision\"},\n42|\t\t{ID: \"path-unmanaged\", Section: \"16.3\", Quote: \"replacement of unmanaged paths\", Gate: \"secprim.Guard.Resolve\"},\n43|\t\t{ID: \"path-device\", Section: \"16.3\", Quote: \"device/FIFO/socket creation\", Gate: \"secprim.CheckMemberPath\"},\n44|\t\t{ID: \"argv-vector\", Section: \"16.4\", Quote: \"Shell command construction from names, paths, provider output, or peer data is forbidden\", Gate: \"secprim.CheckArgv\"},\n45|\t\t{ID: \"env-allowlist\", Section: \"16.7\", Quote: \"minimal environment allowlists\", Gate: \"secprim.IsEnvName\"},\n46|\t\t{ID: \"render-ansi-osc\", Section: \"16.7\", Quote: \"ANSI/OSC sequences\", Gate: \"secprim.EscapeForTerminal\"},\n47|\t\t{ID: \"render-controls\", Section: \"16.7\", Quote: \"control characters\", Gate: \"secprim.EscapeForTerminal\"},\n48|\t\t{ID: \"render-bidi\", Section: \"16.7\", Quote: \"bidi overrides\", Gate: \"secprim.EscapeForTerminal\"},\n49|\t\t{ID: \"render-encoding\", Section: \"16.7\", Quote: \"invalid encodings\", Gate: \"secprim.EscapeForTerminal\"},\n50|\t\t{ID: \"render-width\", Section: \"16.7\", Quote: \"hostile-width graphemes\", Gate: \"secprim.EscapeForTerminal\"},\n51|\t\t{ID: \"redact-persisted\", Section: \"16.4\", Quote: \"redacted before persistence\", Gate: \"secprim.Redact\"},\n52|\t\t{ID: \"redact-credentials\", Section: \"16.2\", Quote: \"API keys, OAuth tokens\", Gate: \"secprim.Redact\"},\n53|\t}\n54|}\n55|\n56|// QuoteInSection reports whether quote occurs on at least one document\n57|// line inside section. The section check is load-bearing, not\n58|// ceremony: TestQuoteInSectionRejectsWrongSection drives a real quote\n59|// against a wrong section and requires refusal, so a mutant that\n60|// preserves the token match but drops the section check fails. The\n61|// mutant harness for that test executes the full behavioral hostile\n62|// battery, not only this checker.\n63|func QuoteInSection(document *specdoc.Document, quote, section string) error {\n64|\tif document == nil {\n65|\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"secconftest: nil specification document is never a legitimate absence\")\n66|\t}\n67|\tif strings.TrimSpace(quote) == \"\" {\n68|\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"secconftest: empty quote matches nothing\")\n69|\t}\n70|\tlines := document.QuoteLines(quote)\n71|\tif len(lines) == 0 {\n72|\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"secconftest: quote %q occurs nowhere in the pinned document\", quote)\n73|\t}\n74|\tfor _, line := range lines {\n75|\t\tif got, ok := document.SectionID(line); ok && got == section {\n76|\t\t\treturn nil\n77|\t\t}\n78|\t}\n79|\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"secconftest: quote %q occurs but never in section %s\", quote, section)\n80|}\n81|\n82|// bidiOverrideRunes enumerates the Section 16.7 bidi class from the\n83|// Unicode code chart, not from the gate under test: embeddings and\n84|// overrides U+202A-U+202E, isolates U+2066-U+2069, directional marks\n85|// U+200E, U+200F, U+061C, and zero-width U+200B-U+200D plus U+FEFF.\n86|// The static list is the point: a gate arm deleted with its test row\n87|// still faces every rune here.\n88|func bidiOverrideRunes() []rune {\n89|\treturn []rune{\n90|\t\t0x202a, 0x202b, 0x202c, 0x202d, 0x202e,\n91|\t\t0x2066, 0x2067, 0x2068, 0x2069,\n92|\t\t0x200e, 0x200f, 0x061c,\n93|\t\t0x200b, 0x200c, 0x200d,\n94|\t\t0xfeff,\n95|\t}\n96|}\n97|\n98|// Members generates the hostile inputs for a roster class ID. Every\n99|// member carries the class token (for example \"..\" for traversal):\n100|// the gates must refuse behavior, not merely match the token, and the\n101|// per-class gate tests assert refusal of every member, so a\n102|// token-preserving behavior change still reddens.\n103|func Members(id string) []string {\n104|\twrap := func(runes []rune) []string {\n105|\t\tmembers := make([]string, 0, len(runes))\n106|\t\tfor _, character := range runes {\n107|\t\t\tmembers = append(members, \"a\"+string(character)+\"b\")\n108|\t\t}\n109|\t\treturn members\n110|\t}\n111|\tswitch id {\n112|\tcase \"path-traversal\":\n113|\t\treturn []string{\"../evil\", \"a/../../evil\", \"..\", \"a/../b\", \"a/b/../../../../c\", \"....//evil\"}\n114|\tcase \"path-absolute\":\n115|\t\treturn []string{\"/etc/passwd\", \"/\", \"/a/b\"}\n116|\tcase \"path-drive-unc\":\n117|\t\treturn []string{\"C:/Windows\", \"C:relative\", \"d:foo\", \"//server/share\", `\\\\server\\share`}\n118|\tcase \"path-altstream\":\n119|\t\treturn []string{\"file:stream\", \"dir/file:ads\", \"file:$DATA\"}\n120|\tcase \"path-encoded-separator\":\n121|\t\treturn []string{\"a%2fb\", \"a%5cb\", \"a%252fb\", \"a%2F..%2Fb\", \"%2e%2e/evil\", \"a/%2e%2e/b\", \"%2E%2E\", \"a%c0%afb\", \"a%C0%AFb\"}\n122|\tcase \"path-case-collision\":\n123|\t\treturn []string{\"readme.md\", \"README.MD\", \"ReadMe.Md\", \"STAGING/MEMBER\", \"Staging/Member\"}\n124|\tcase \"path-unmanaged\":\n125|\t\treturn []string{\"outside-managed\", \"staging/other\", \"secret\"}\n126|\tcase \"path-device\":\n127|\t\treturn []string{\"NUL\", \"CON\", \"COM1\", \"LPT1.txt\", \"aux.log\", \"dir/PRN\", \"COM9\", \"nul\"}\n128|\tcase \"argv-vector\":\n129|\t\treturn []string{\"; rm -rf /\", \"$(evil)\", \"`evil`\", \"a|b\", \"a&&b\", \"a\\nb\", \"--flag=value with spaces\"}\n130|\tcase \"env-allowlist\":\n131|\t\treturn []string{\"\", \"0\", \"1ABC\", \"A-B\", \"A B\", \"A.B\", \"A/B\", \"A\\x00B\", strings.Repeat(\"A\", 129)}\n132|\tcase \"render-ansi-osc\":\n133|\t\treturn []string{\"a\\x1b[31mb\", \"a\\x1b[0mb\", \"a\\x1b]8;;https://evil\\x07b\", \"a\\x9bb\"}\n134|\tcase \"render-controls\":\n135|\t\t// C0 controls except newline and tab (which the gate keeps),\n136|\t\t// DEL, and C1 controls: the exact refused set the section\n137|\t\t// names as \"control characters\". Printable runes are not\n138|\t\t// hostile and are not members.\n139|\t\tvar runes []rune\n140|\t\tfor character := rune(0); character < 0x20; character++ {\n141|\t\t\tif character == '\\n' || character == '\\t' {\n142|\t\t\t\tcontinue\n143|\t\t\t}\n144|\t\t\trunes = append(runes, character)\n145|\t\t}\n146|\t\trunes = append(runes, 0x7f)\n147|\t\tfor character := rune(0x80); character < 0xa0; character++ {\n148|\t\t\trunes = append(runes, character)\n149|\t\t}\n150|\t\treturn wrap(runes)\n151|\tcase \"render-bidi\":\n152|\t\treturn wrap([]rune{0x202a, 0x202b, 0x202c, 0x202d, 0x202e, 0x2066, 0x2067, 0x2068, 0x2069})\n153|\tcase \"render-encoding\":\n154|\t\treturn []string{\"a\\xffb\", \"a\\xfeb\", \"\\xff\", \"\\xfe\", \"a\\xed\\xa0\\x80b\"}\n155|\tcase \"render-width\":\n156|\t\treturn wrap([]rune{0x200e, 0x200f, 0x061c, 0x200b, 0x200c, 0x200d, 0xfeff})\n157|\tcase \"redact-persisted\":\n158|\t\treturn []string{\n159|\t\t\t\"-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nMIIB\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\",\n160|\t\t\t\"key without end -----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nMIIB\",\n161|\t\t\t\"https://deploy:s3cret@example.com/repo\",\n162|\t\t\t\"password=hunter2\",\n163|\t\t}\n164|\tcase \"redact-credentials\":\n165|\t\treturn []string{\n166|\t\t\t\"api_key=[REDACTED]\",\n167|\t\t\t\"authorization: Bearer [REDACTED]\",\n168|\t\t\t`\"token\": \"[REDACTED]\"`,\n169|\t\t\t\"cookie=sessionid=abc123\",\n170|\t\t\t\"subscription_token=st_live_42\",\n171|\t\t}\n172|\tdefault:\n173|\t\treturn nil\n174|\t}\n175|}\n176|\n177|// BidiRunes exposes the full bidi/zero-width rune list for gate tests\n178|// that must assert the enumerated set without retyping it.\n179|func BidiRunes() []rune {\n180|\treturn bidiOverrideRunes()\n181|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c52d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a077c8-d88a-7931-843c-3e9dddbe9444"},"sequence":241,"recorded_at":1780531400000477,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68"},"call_id":"call_01a077c94e0b7dc2a210474fdf303b12","text":"Read text file `internal/secconftest/runner.go`.\n1|package secconftest\n2|\n3|import (\n4|\t\"fmt\"\n5|\t\"hash/fnv\"\n6|\t\"sort\"\n7|\t\"sync/atomic\"\n8|)\n9|\n10|// Calls counts production entry-point invocations. A fixture receives a\n11|// counter and must record every production call it makes; the Runner\n12|// refuses fixtures that record none, so a corpus that never reaches the\n13|// entry point cannot pass as coverage.\n14|type Calls struct {\n15|\tcount atomic.Int64\n16|}\n17|\n18|// Add records n production calls.\n19|func (calls *Calls) Add(n int64) {\n20|\tcalls.count.Add(n)\n21|}\n22|\n23|// Count reports the recorded calls.\n24|func (calls *Calls) Count() int64 {\n25|\treturn calls.count.Load()\n26|}\n27|\n28|// Fixture is one named deterministic case. Run must be a pure function\n29|// of Seed: same seed, same report string. It receives a fresh Calls\n30|// counter and must record each production entry-point invocation it\n31|// performs.\n32|type Fixture struct {\n33|\tName string\n34|\tRun  func(seed uint64, calls *Calls) (report string, err error)\n35|}\n36|\n37|// Result is the executed record of one fixture.\n38|type Result struct {\n39|\tName   string\n40|\tReport string\n41|\tCalls  int64\n42|}\n43|\n44|// Runner executes fixtures in sorted name order over an explicit seed.\n45|// Registration order and Go map iteration order never affect execution:\n46|// names are sorted before every run, so identical inputs produce one\n47|// answer no matter how the caller stored the fixtures.\n48|type Runner struct {\n49|\t// AllowUnreached disables the arrival check. It exists only so the\n50|\t// suite can prove the check is load-bearing: with it set, an\n51|\t// unreached fixture passes, and TestRunnerRefusesUnreachedFixture\n52|\t// pins that the default refuses. Production use leaves the zero\n53|\t// value.\n54|\tAllowUnreached bool\n55|}\n56|\n57|// Run executes every fixture once and returns results in sorted name\n58|// order. A fixture that records zero production calls is refused with\n59|// an error naming it, unless AllowUnreached is set.\n60|func (runner Runner) Run(fixtures []Fixture, seed uint64) ([]Result, error) {\n61|\tordered := append([]Fixture(nil), fixtures...)\n62|\tsort.Slice(ordered, func(i, j int) bool { return ordered[i].Name < ordered[j].Name })\n63|\tresults := make([]Result, 0, len(ordered))\n64|\tfor _, fixture := range ordered {\n65|\t\tvar calls Calls\n66|\t\treport, err := fixture.Run(seed, &calls)\n67|\t\tif err != nil {\n68|\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"secconftest runner: fixture %q: %w\", fixture.Name, err)\n69|\t\t}\n70|\t\tif !runner.AllowUnreached && calls.Count() == 0 {\n71|\t\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"secconftest runner: fixture %q recorded no production call\", fixture.Name)\n72|\t\t}\n73|\t\tresults = append(results, Result{Name: fixture.Name, Report: report, Calls: calls.Count()})\n74|\t}\n75|\treturn results, nil\n76|}\n77|\n78|// Digest folds the execution log into one FNV-64 hex value: fixture\n79|// names, reports, and call counts in execution order. Two runs with\n80|// identical inputs produce identical digests; any dropped, reordered,\n81|// or altered fixture changes it.\n82|func Digest(results []Result) string {\n83|\tsum := fnv.New64a()\n84|\tfor _, result := range results {\n85|\t\t_, _ = fmt.Fprintf(sum, \"%s\\x00%s\\x00%d\\x00\", result.Name, result.Report, result.Calls)\n86|\t}\n87|\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"%016x\", sum.Sum64())\n88|}\n89|\n90|// SortedNames reports the execution order for a fixture list. It shares\n91|// the sort with Run so the order claim is testable in isolation.\n92|func SortedNames(fixtures []Fixture) []string {\n93|\tordered := append([]Fixture(nil), fixtures...)\n94|\tsort.Slice(ordered, func(i, j int) bool { return ordered[i].Name < ordered[j].Name })\n95|\tnames := make([]string, 0, len(ordered))\n96|\tfor _, fixture := range ordered {\n97|\t\tnames = append(names, fixture.Name)\n98|\t}\n99|\treturn names\n100|}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
