{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000caef","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a077c8-d88a-7931-843c-3e9dddbe9444"},"sequence":978,"recorded_at":1780531400001951,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d502244e-0589-4672-9c20-02af4f4b0b68"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"adfe4a49-3ef0-4288-b343-5e1373b53454"},"task_id":"adfe4a49-3ef0-4288-b343-5e1373b53454","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"adfe4a49-3ef0-4288-b343-5e1373b53454","chunk":"Read text file `internal/secconftest/doc.go`.\n1|// Package secconftest is the conformance-test instrument for the\n2|// security-primitives-and-conformance-ci story: deterministic fixture\n3|// runners, fake clocks, crash points, protocol fuzz drivers, hostile\n4|// strings, and platform capability skips over the Section 16 production\n5|// gates (internal/secprim).\n6|//\n7|// It is an instrument, not a second authority. Every behavior it covers\n8|// is decided by an existing owner; this package only drives those\n9|// decisions deterministically and proves the drive reaches them:\n10|//\n11|//   - path grammars and bounds stay in internal/scalar;\n12|//   - wire decoding, string measure, and bound checks stay in\n13|//     internal/environ;\n14|//   - argv, environment, redaction, escaping, and containment refusals\n15|//     stay in internal/secprim, whose production code this package must\n16|//     not change;\n17|//   - the pinned specification text stays in internal/specdoc, which\n18|//     this package reads only to derive hostile classes, never to\n19|//     advertise product capabilities.\n20|//\n21|// Normative scope: AX v0.5.0 Sections 16, 19.2-19.5, 20.2, Appendix D.\n22|// Section 19.3 provider suites and Section 19.4 lifecycle cases outside\n23|// Section 16 are not driven here; the per-mechanism tests name that\n24|// bound where it bites.\n25|//\n26|// Stated bounds. The crash Transactor is a test model of a three-phase\n27|// durable commit, not the product journal: it proves the injector and\n28|// the outcome vocabulary work, not that any product recovery path\n29|// works. The five crash-point names are instrument-local phase\n30|// boundaries, not the SPEC CR-MAT-01..08 registry: registry-style\n31|// names are refused as unknown, and a new crash site added without an\n32|// arm is outside this model. Every arm the suite sets is\n33|// consumption-checked through RemainingArmed, so an armed point the\n34|// drive never reaches fails its test instead of passing silently. The\n35|// fuzz drivers cover the Section 16 string and vector gates only;\n36|// containment races and TOCTOU behavior are out of scope for\n37|// byte-mutating fuzz. The IsEnvName and Redact fuzz properties pin\n38|// the two poles a no-op gate fails (refused empty/admitted\n39|// well-formed name; rewritten secret/passing innocuous line); the\n40|// interior grammar and per-class markers stay covered by the hostile\n41|// corpus tests named on each property. Hostile members derive from the\n42|// specification text plus package-unicode tables and internal/scalar\n43|// predicates, so deleting a secprim gate arm keeps the corpus and\n44|// reddens the gate test instead of shrinking the witness with it. The\n45|// per-test skip datum is the `--- SKIP` lines under `go test -v`,\n46|// not the process-wide Report line, which only honestly witnesses\n47|// the verdicts the suite really recorded.\n48|package secconftest"}}}
